response:
 First, notice $AE<AB$ and $BE<BC$ , so $E$ must lie on the inside of $\triangle ABC$ with $A$ as a  vertex . Next I will construct an altitude and apply the Pythagorean Theorem. Suppose $E$ lies $x$ units away from side $AD$ . Considering the altitude to $BC$ whose length must equal its counterpart to $AD$ , two similar triangles are formed. 

[asy] draw((0,0)--(4,0)--(4,4)--(0,4)--cycle); draw((4,0)--(-1,5)--(7,5)--(12,0)); dot((5,5)); label("E", (5,5), N); dot((0,5)); label("F", (0,5), W); dot((4.5, 0)); label("B", (4.5, 0), S); dot((4,4)); label("C", (4,4), NE); dot((4,0)); label("B", (4,0), S); dot((4,-.5)); label("B", (4,-.5), S); dot((5,0)); label("D", (5,0), S); dot((7,5)); label("A", (7,5), N); dot((12,4)); label("P", (12,4), N); dot((11,0)); label("P'", (11,0), S); draw((7,5)--(11,0), dashed); draw((11,0)--(12,4), dashed); [/asy] Therefore $\triangle ABE \sim \triangle ECF$. We have $AB=13, AE=5, BE=12, BC=13$ and $CF=13-x$. Using the Pythagorean Theorem, \begin{align*} 5^2+x^2 &= 12^2 \\ x^2 &= 119 \\ x &\approx 10.9 \end{align*} Since $BE < BC$ and $AE < AB$, $E$ can wait at most $AB$ units to the left of $A$ and $BC$ units above $B$. That means $E$ must lie within the square in question.

