 so we need to determine $n$? $1000$ complicates things. Let’s try and find a way to cancel some instances of $f(i)$ on both sides</think>
\begin{align*}
&1000 f(1^2) f(2^2) \dotsm f(n^2) \\
= ~ &1000 \prod_{i=1}^{n} f(i^2) \\
= ~ &1000 \prod_{i=1}^{n} (i^4 + i^2 + 1 ).
\end{align*}
If we factor $i^4 + i^2 + 1$ we get $(i^3-i+1)(i^3+i+1).$  Hence, the LHS becomes \begin{align*}
1000 \prod_{i=1}^{n} (i^4 + i^2 + 1) &= 1000 \prod_{i=1}^{n} (i^4 + i^2 + 1) \\
&= 1000 \prod_{i=1}^{n} (i^3-i+1)(i^3+i+1).
\end{align*}
On the RHS of the inequality, we also have a product of  factors, whose factors are presented below.
\[
\begin{align*}
f(1^2) &= 1^4 + 1^2 + 1 \\
&=2(1^3+1+1) \\
&=2(1+1+1)
\end{align*}
\begin{align*}
f(2^2) &= 2^4 + 2^2 + 1 \\
&=(2^3-2+1)(2^3+2+1) & \text{ but first factor is actually $2$ times bigger, so we divide out $2$} \\
&=(8-2+1)(8+2+1) \\
&=7 \cdot 11 \\
&=7(2^3-2+1)
\end{align*} \begin{align*}
f(3^2) &= 3^4 + 3^2 + 1 \\
&=(3^3-3+1)(3^3+3+1) \\
&=(27-3+1)(27+3+1) \\
&=25 \cdot 31 \\
&=25 (3^3 -3+1) \\
\end{align*}
\begin{align*}
&\dotsm 
\end{align*}
Now the product on the RHS can be written as \[
\prod_{i=1}^{n} f(i^2) = \prod_{i=1}^{n} (2 \cdot (i^3-i+1))(i^3+i+1) = \prod_{i=1}^{n} 2(i^3-i+1) (i^3+i+1).
\] We see that $1000 \prod_{i=1}^{n} (i^4 + i^2 + 1)$ need only be multiplied by $(\frac{1}{2})^{2n}$ to become equal to $\prod_{i=1}^{n} 2(i^3-i+1) (i^3+i+1).$  Hence we have \begin{align*}
(2n)^2 \cdot 1000 \prod_{i=1}^{n} (i^4+i^2+1) &\ge \prod_{i=1}^{n} 2(i^3-i+1)(i^3+i+1) \\
\Rightarrow 4000n^2  \prod_{i=1}^{n} (i^4+i^2+1) &\ge \prod_{i=1}^{n} 2(i^3-i+1)(i^3+i+1).
\end{align*}
We must therefore have $n \ge \boxed{16}.$ <answer>

